  "answer": "Gradients aren't part of this course, so I can't point you to anything in our materials about them — that topic belongs to a later course, not Calculus I. What we do here is the derivative of a single-variable function, and the closest idea we have is the derivative as a rate of change and as the slope of the tangent line.\n\nNot from your course materials: a gradient is essentially the multivariable version of the derivative — a vector of partial derivatives that points in the direction of steepest increase. You'd meet it after learning partial derivatives.\n\nFor now, it's worth being solid on the single-variable picture, since that's what everything later builds on. Can you tell me what the derivative of a function at a point means geometrically, and how it's defined as a limit?",
